AZ-104: Manage Identities and Governance in Azure

Platform: Microsoft
Course: AZ-104: Manage Identities and Governance in Azure
Level: Beginner
Role Path: Azure Administrator
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn how to manage identities, access control, subscriptions, and governance tools within Microsoft Azure. This learning path focuses on securing Azure environments using identity management, Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), resource governance, and monitoring tools commonly used by Azure administrators.

Step 1: Introduction to Identity and Governance

The learning path introduced the importance of identity management and governance in cloud environments.

Main objectives of Azure governance:

Secure access to resources
Control permissions
Maintain compliance
Organize cloud resources
Monitor usage and costs
Enforce organizational policies

The course emphasized that identity and governance are foundational components of cloud security.

Key takeaway:
Proper governance ensures Azure resources remain secure, organized, and compliant with organizational policies.

Step 2: Managing Microsoft Entra ID Objects

The module introduced Microsoft Entra ID and explained how Azure administrators manage identities.

Topics covered:

Users
Groups
Administrative units
Devices
User authentication methods

Tasks learned:

Creating and managing users
Assigning users to groups
Resetting passwords
Managing group memberships
Configuring user settings

The course also explained synchronization between on-premises Active Directory and Microsoft Entra ID.

Key takeaway:
Microsoft Entra ID centralizes authentication and identity management for Azure environments.

Step 3: Authentication and Access Management

The course covered authentication and access security features.

Concepts learned:

Authentication vs authorization
Single Sign-On (SSO)
Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)
Conditional Access
Self-Service Password Reset (SSPR)

Conditional Access policies help organizations enforce security requirements based on:

User location
Device compliance
Risk level
Application access

Key takeaway:
Strong authentication policies improve cloud security and reduce the risk of unauthorized access.

Step 4: Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)

The learning path introduced Azure Role-based access control.

RBAC allows administrators to control resource access using predefined or custom roles.

Important concepts:

Principle of least privilege
Scope inheritance
Built-in roles
Custom roles

Common built-in roles:

Owner
Contributor
Reader
User Access Administrator

The course demonstrated how permissions can be assigned at different scopes:

Management group
Subscription
Resource group
Individual resource

Key takeaway:
RBAC helps organizations securely manage permissions while minimizing unnecessary access.

Step 5: Managing Azure Subscriptions and Resource Organization

The module explained how Azure organizes resources across subscriptions and management groups.

Topics covered:

Azure subscriptions
Management groups
Resource groups
Resource organization strategies

The course highlighted the importance of separating environments such as:

Production
Development
Testing

Benefits of structured organization:

Easier administration
Better cost tracking
Improved security management
Simplified governance

Key takeaway:
Logical resource organization improves operational efficiency and governance.

Step 6: Azure Resource Manager (ARM)

The learning path introduced Azure Resource Manager.

ARM is the deployment and management framework for Azure resources.

Capabilities include:

Resource deployment
Infrastructure as Code (IaC)
Template-based deployments
Dependency management
Consistent resource provisioning

The module explained ARM templates and how they automate infrastructure deployment.

Key takeaway:
Azure Resource Manager enables scalable and repeatable cloud deployments.

Step 7: Governance with Azure Policy and Resource Locks

The course explored governance tools used to enforce organizational standards.

Topics covered:

Azure Policy
Resource Locks
Tags

Azure Policy examples:

Restricting allowed regions
Enforcing naming conventions
Requiring resource tags
Controlling VM sizes

Resource Locks help prevent:

Accidental deletion
Unauthorized modifications

Types of locks:

Delete lock
Read-only lock

Key takeaway:
Governance tools help maintain compliance and reduce operational risks.

Step 8: Cost Management and Monitoring

The learning path introduced Azure cost management and monitoring features.

Tools covered:

Azure Cost Management
Budgets and alerts
Azure Monitor
Activity Logs

Administrators can:

Track resource spending
Analyze usage trends
Configure cost alerts
Monitor system activity

The course emphasized proactive monitoring to maintain efficient cloud operations.

Key takeaway:
Cost monitoring and activity tracking are essential parts of Azure administration.

Skills Learned
Managing users and groups in Microsoft Entra ID
Configuring authentication and MFA
Implementing Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)
Organizing Azure subscriptions and resource groups
Using Azure Resource Manager (ARM)
Applying Azure Policy and resource locks
Monitoring Azure activity and costs
Understanding cloud governance principles
Conclusion

The AZ-104: Manage Identities and Governance in Azure learning path provided foundational knowledge for securing and governing Azure environments.

The course focused heavily on identity management, access control, governance policies, and subscription organization using Microsoft Entra ID and Azure management tools.

These concepts are critical for Azure administrators and cybersecurity professionals because identity and governance form the foundation of cloud security and operational control within enterprise Azure environments.
